<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<domain-models xmlns="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models
    http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models/domain-models_7.4.xsd">

  <module name="aiaccountant" package="com.axelor.apps.aiaccountant.db"/>

  <!-- Operational toggles an administrator may change without a redeploy.

    WHY IT EXISTS. Env vars and AppSettings alone mean a container restart to turn the assistant
    off, to stop it writing, or to promote the router. Those three are exactly the switches somebody
    needs during an incident, when a redeploy is the last thing they want to be waiting for.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. Endpoints, tenant id, client id and the client secret. Those are
    infrastructure credentials: they belong in the container's environment, not in a table that a
    form view can render. Nothing in this entity is a secret, and nothing that is a secret may be
    added to it.

    TRI-STATE, NOT BOOLEAN. Each toggle is a selection whose default is INHERIT rather than a boolean
    defaulting to false. A boolean cannot distinguish "the administrator turned this off" from "no
    administrator has ever touched this", so the moment the row existed every toggle would read as
    off and the deployment's own configuration would be silently overridden. INHERIT falls through to
    the existing env -> AppSettings -> default chain, unchanged.

    SINGLETON. One row, enforced by the unique `singletonKey` rather than by convention: stock
    Axelor's own AppBase relies on data-init seeding exactly one row and nothing stops a second, which
    is a reasonable risk for a config nobody writes to twice and an unreasonable one for a row that
    decides whether the assistant may write to the books. The row is created only when an
    administrator first opens the Settings screen — never by a config read; see
    AiAccountantSettingOverrideService. -->
  <entity name="AiAccountantSetting">

    <!-- The whole point of this column is its unique constraint. Its value is always "default". -->
    <string name="singletonKey" title="Key" required="true" unique="true" max="32"
      default="default"/>

    <integer name="enabledSelect" title="Assistant enabled"
      selection="aiaccountant.override.tristate.select" default="0"/>

    <integer name="routerEnabledSelect" title="Router enabled"
      selection="aiaccountant.override.tristate.select" default="0"/>

    <integer name="routerModeSelect" title="Router mode"
      selection="aiaccountant.override.router.mode.select" default="0"/>

    <!-- A guard flag beside the value rather than a nullable Integer: `default="false"` means "do not
      override", which is the safe reading of an untouched field, and it avoids depending on whether
      the generator boxes a bare <integer>. -->
    <boolean name="overrideMaxToolIterations" title="Override max tool iterations" default="false"/>

    <integer name="maxToolIterations" title="Max tool iterations"/>

    <!-- Stores the chosen preset code (see AiConversationMemoryPreset), never a token count — the
      Settings screen never lets anyone type one in. Deliberately no SQL-level column default: an
      existing database gets NULL for this new column, and AiConversationMemoryPreset.fromStored
      reads that (and any value it does not recognise) as MEDIUM, so no migration is needed. The
      `default="3"` below only seeds a brand-new row's Java object (opened for the first time),
      so a fresh Settings screen shows "Medium" selected instead of a blank field. -->
    <integer name="memoryPresetSelect" title="Conversation memory"
      selection="aiaccountant.memory.preset.select" default="3"/>

  </entity>

</domain-models>
